{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create the opsbot account on node1 and enable opsbot on node1 to execute commands remotely as opsbot on node2 using an SSH key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsbot was created on both nodes, an Ed25519 key was generated under opsbot on node1, the matching public key was installed for opsbot on node2, and final post-restart testing executed commands on node2 as opsbot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8108ba1b3cca4f93","cmd-b59bf43865c04567","cmd-249e915d581f4063","cmd-24504351f2954c06","cmd-78ef720a9a4948dd"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Require key-based SSH authentication for opsbot on both node1 and node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Effective sshd policy on both nodes requires publickey authentication and disables keyboard-interactive authentication; earlier direct checks also show password authentication disabled. Node2 behavior was directly tested: key login succeeded and a password-only attempt was rejected with Permission denied (publickey). The policy and access survived both restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fab1288dec514308","cmd-6d1cefeb74c842bd","cmd-10022484b2524333","cmd-9c819bd8534c4b97","cmd-78ef720a9a4948dd"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the existing platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 before and after execution. The implementation used the platform's installed OpenSSH and standard system utilities without changing packages or the kernel.","evidence_ids":["cmd-dc1443e91a44453e","cmd-f7566bff2d8c4a92","cmd-10022484b2524333","cmd-9c819bd8534c4b97","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Account creation, password locking, SSH policy files, key material, authorized_keys, known_hosts pinning, SELinux restoration, and sshd reloads directly supported the task. The two reboots and their transient systemd units were required by the provider-wide restart protocol. Temporary files were trap-cleaned, and final snapshots show no failed services, damaged package state, unexpected listeners, or abandoned task files. Both restart protocols were complete: each node had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/configuration/data state; fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8108ba1b3cca4f93","cmd-b59bf43865c04567","cmd-249e915d581f4063","cmd-24504351f2954c06","cmd-9550ec26cea045fc","cmd-5385f6dfb0904ad2","cmd-c7e5ad377c7442c7","cmd-10022484b2524333","cmd-9c819bd8534c4b97","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. opsbot has persistent key-based command access from node1 to node2, key-only policy applies to opsbot on both systems, and the original RHEL release and kernel were preserved. Provider-wide restart evidence is complete and coherent for both nodes.","limitations":["No inbound SSH login was behaviorally exercised against node1; its key-only result is established through effective sshd policy inspection before and after restart.","Some later password-status and passwordauthentication fields were redacted or malformed in captured output. Earlier unredacted effective-policy checks, AuthenticationMethods publickey, locked-password evidence, and node2's negative authentication test compensate for this."]}
